	<h2> Artist Statement </h2>

	<p>
		Confusion is often associated with negative connotative thinking, yet it consists of quite the prevalence of today. I create multimodality interactive digital artworks that often incorporate elements of whimsy and quirkiness in prompting how we respond to foreign circumstances and overchoice. Interactive immersive experiences; like videogames or AR/VR, often allow the audience into a safe and simplified domain for experimentation and failure. My works sometimes intend to confuse and subvert the audience, but in a fun way. I love the nuances behind the adaptability of metacommunication in which we choose to conduct ourselves to one another.
	</p>

	<p>
		My process consists of a tug and pull game theory-akin like relationship of audience anticipation. The thoughtline chaining produces arbitrary hypothetical situations that ask; what is my intended impact, and how do I think they will react? I want to express whimsical metacommunication that can allow the avenues of interpretation for audience self-observation. What led you to playing or experiencing a artwork? What led you to reading this artist statement, and does that matter, or rather, matter to you? “I don’t know” sits at the root behind overwhelming choice. At the end of the day, I’m just interested in making fun little games/experiences for others to enjoy, and experimenting with ideas on whimsical metacommunication. 
	</p>

	<h2> Biography </h2>
	<p>
		I’m Caden McMullen, a senior Creative Technologies student at Virginia Tech. I’ve learned a vast variety of fundamental skills and concepts in both technical and creative production. I primarily work on projects that involve; programming, 2D and 3D animation, interactive media, and sound. I grew up in Atalanta, Georgia. I have family based in North Georgia and Texas. I’ve always had a love for videogames ever since first experiencing them with my friends and family growing up. They were the main medium for how I developed and maintained relationships with friends and my family. Which is part of my aspiration(s) of creating interactive experiences that heavily incorporate elements of accessibility and collaboration/community. 
	</p>

	<p>
		I want to create games/experiences that can appeal to wider audiences and communities. I currently aim to broaden my flexibility with the skills I learn in order to improve my generalist skillset. I am also interested in experimental approaches and concepts in process creation. My current focus is trying to create more completed projects that utilize a wide mix of various technical applications across multiple digital mediums, into coherent products. One project I am working towards is programming an interactive web and mobile app that consists of an anagram-like word game. I intend to curate the game for my Grandmother, testing and creating features from her feedback. Another project I’m working toward is a co-op 3D rouge-like platform fighter multiplayer videogame. I’m also working toward learning more about music and sound production in DAWs. Part of my desire in learning all of these tools and skills is to be able to be flexible when it comes to working collaboratively in creating. Having the ability to fill in the slots that are needed in a creation pipeline when necessary. I already have some experience creating projects with fellow classmates, which also includes game jams.
	</p>

